  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "For the lowercase English letter α\\alphaα, let ord(α)\\text{ord}(\\alpha)ord(α) denote the order of α\\alphaα in the alphabet, under the standard ordering a,b,c,…,za, b, c, \\ldots, za,b,c,…,z.\n\nFor example, ord(b)=2,ord(j)=10,\\text{ord}(b) = 2, \\text{ord}(j) = 10,ord(b)=2,ord(j)=10, and ord(z)=26\\text{ord}(z) = 26ord(z)=26.\n\nYou are given a string SSS containing NNN lowercase English letters.\n\nYou can perform the following 222 operations on it:\n\n\nChoose a subsequence of the string that equals \"back\"\\texttt{\"back\"}\"back\", and delete the subsequence from SSS.\n\nThen, choose any character α\\alphaα such that ord(α)\\text{ord}(\\alpha)ord(α) has **at most** two divisors, and insert α\\alphaα at the start of SSS.\n\nNote that this operation reduces the length of SSS by 333.\n\n\nChoose a subsequence of the string that equals \"front\"\\texttt{\"front\"}\"front\", and delete the subsequence from SSS.\n\nThen, choose any character α\\alphaα such that ord(α)\\text{ord}(\\alpha)ord(α) has **strictly more than** two divisors, and append α\\alphaα to SSS.\n\nNote that this operation reduces the length of SSS by 444.\n\n\nYou may perform these two operations as many times as you like (possibly, zero), and in any order.\n\nFind the **minimum** possible length of the final string after doing so.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input contains a single integer TTT — the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the size of the string.\nThe second line of each test case contains the string SSS, consisting of lowercase English letters.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum possible length of resulting string.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\leq T \\leq 10^41≤T≤104",
      "1≤N≤3⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 3\\cdot 10^51≤N≤3⋅105",
      "SSS contains only lowercase English letters, i.e, the characters `a, b, c, ..., z`.",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 3⋅1053\\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n15\nefmbagrowcnukpt\n11\nbxabcacfkck",
        "output": "8\n5",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Consider the following sequence of operations:\n\nInitially, S=\"efmbagrowcnukpt\"S = \\texttt{\"efmbagrowcnukpt\"}S=\"efmbagrowcnukpt\".\nIndices 4,5,10,134, 5, 10, 134,5,10,13 form a subsequence equal to \"back\"\\texttt{\"back\"}\"back\".\nDelete that, and insert qqq at the start of SSS. Note that ord(q)=17\\text{ord}(q) = 17ord(q)=17, which has only two divisors.\nThe new string is S=\"qefmgrownupt\"S = \\texttt{\"qefmgrownupt\"}S=\"qefmgrownupt\".\nNow, the indices 3,6,7,9,123,6,7,9,123,6,7,9,12 form a subsequence equal to \"front\"\\texttt{\"front\"}\"front\".\nDelete it and append ddd to SSS, turning it into \"qemgwupd\"\\texttt{\"qemgwupd\"}\"qemgwupd\".\n\nSSS now has length 888. It can be shown that reaching a smaller length is not possible."
      }
    ]
  },
